From: skornuta <126527569+skornuta@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 11:57:53 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 03/27] victory-gpu: detile textures on the card
 (VICTORY_GPU_DETILE=1, off)

With the switch a title texture has no staging buffer of its own. A list
that uploads it reserves a slice of a ring of upload memory for the title
bytes and one of a ring in the card's memory for the detiled texture,
laid out as its copyable footprints; its CopyTextureRegions read the
latter. At submission the title memory is read and hashed as the refresh
reads it, copied into the raw slice, and a compute shader copies it by
table (`Layout::copy_table`, packed: runs of at most 64 bytes, cached by
layout, mip and pitches; an array slice of a thin layout is slice 0's
table moved by a root constant) in a list that goes ahead of the
submission's lists in the same call. The ring rests in the common state:
COMMON -> UAV, the dispatches, UAV -> COPY_SOURCE (readbacks for
verify), COPY_SOURCE -> COMMON, batched once per submission.

Never waits, never overruns: both rings have a fixed size
(VICTORY_GPU_DETILE_RAW_MB 256, VICTORY_GPU_DETILE_OUT_MB 192, made only
with room under 85 % of the card's budget, halved down to 32 MB to find
it; VICTORY_GPU_DETILE_TABLE_MB 32). Slices are first-fit in 64 KiB
grains and go back when the list lets the upload go and the card has
passed every submission that used it. A refusal (a ring full, the table
buffer full, a table not in whole 32-bit words, a table reaching outside
its slices) sends the upload the CPU's way at once: the staging buffer is
made then, kept for the staging state's life, and its first refresh
detiles whatever the memory says. Every table is bounded on the CPU and
every word again in the shader. A list submitted again while its last
submission may read its slices leaves them as they are.

VICTORY_GPU_DETILE_VERIFY=<n>: n uploads a second are read back and
compared with the CPU's detile of the same bytes. A status line every
ten seconds counts uploads, dispatches, ring use and every refusal.

Without the switch every staging state gets its buffer when it is made
and the route returns the CPU's way before asking: the path is the old.

Tests: the arena (full, out-of-order release by fence), packed tables
emulated as the shader runs them against `detile` (slices by shift
included), the shader itself on WARP, every part of real BC7 and RGBA
footprints inside its slices (the last subresource's short last row),
and a texture whose staging buffer was let go coming back with the rings
full: refused at once, nothing half reserved, a staging buffer made, and
the next refresh detiles although the memory is unchanged.

(cherry picked from commit 0ab9ab8c2c90c53ebd244adbddcf7a97ee25103e)
